implementationPlan.description:
The fix, identical in both tools. In packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/qaApprove.ts:127 and packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/qaReject.ts:151, replace
  const handoffWorkerId = task.assignedWorkerId || params.workerId;
with exactly (same indentation as the line it replaces):
  // Idle only the seat that held this row: its assignee. On an unassigned
  // row, where assertWorkerOwns lets any seat give the verdict, that is the
  // caller only when its own pointer is on this row, so a seat that holds
  // another task keeps its claim.
  const handoffWorkerId = task.assignedWorkerId
    || (params.workerId && state.getWorker(params.workerId)?.currentTaskId === task.id ? params.workerId : null);
The dry-run diff is /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-fefce964/fix.diff (2 files, +12/-2). Use your own editor (Edit, apply_patch, or Serena replace_content), never a shell write.

Why this shape:
- Keep `||`, not `??`: an empty assignee or workerId stays 'none', as assertWorkerOwns reads it.
- Read the worker at this line, before updateTask. Both verdicts run under the global state mutex. updateTask never rewrites the caller's record on an unassigned row: its prior-owner release (state/taskStore.ts:622-639) keys on task.assignedWorkerId.
- touchWorker (state/workerStore.ts:91-116) already no-ops a null id, a missing record and a DEAD one. So no new error handling is needed, and no refusal changes.

Leave untouched:
- a5157059's needsHumanReview fence (qaApprove.ts:74-81, qaReject.ts:137-144);
- the finalizing hold, assertWorkerOwns and assertContextFetched;
- the touchWorker lines (qaApprove.ts:258-260, qaReject.ts:391-393) and their payload;
- the tool description strings and every other line.

Placement: in qaReject.ts change only the line at :151. task-77e54ebb (PLANNING) edits the cap-escalation block (:199-245); keep your hunk away from it so merge-tree lands both.

Adversarial self-review, the final step. Load the adversarial-self-review skill and read your diff as an attacker.
- Identity: the two handoffWorkerId hunks, comment included, are byte-identical. Diff the two hunks against each other.
- Precedence: the needsHumanReview fence, qa_approve's finalizing hold, assertWorkerOwns, assertContextFetched and the summary/candidate/delivery gates all still run before any write. Nothing moved.
- Walk every caller shape:
  - assigned row, its owner calls: owner idled, as before;
  - assigned row, no workerId: assignee idled;
  - assigned row, another seat calls: NOT_ALLOWED, unchanged;
  - unassigned row, caller busy elsewhere: untouched;
  - unassigned row, caller's pointer on it: idled;
  - unassigned row, IDLE caller with no task: untouched (it was already IDLE/null);
  - unknown workerId: getWorker is null, so no touch (touchWorker skipped a missing record before too);
  - DEAD caller: touchWorker skips DEAD;
  - parked row with a workerId: refused, unchanged;
  - qa_reject's hard-cap park of an assigned row: captured assignee idled, unchanged (no updateTask prior-owner release there, because the status does not change);
  - empty-string workerId: falsy, so the human path, as before.
- Concurrency: neither tool is `blocking` and neither takes its own runExclusive, so McpAdapter runs the whole handler under the global state mutex. The getWorker read and the touchWorker write cannot interleave with another tool.
- Scope: no other line changed in either tool, nothing in qaReject.ts's :199-245 cap block (task-77e54ebb), no docs, no description string.
- The test bites: it fails on the unfixed code (your step-1 RED). It also fails on the 'touch no one' mutant `const handoffWorkerId = task.assignedWorkerId;`, which fails the two (b) cases; the architect's dry run gave 2 failed / 235 passed.

If anything changes, re-run the step-3 checkRef and submit that fresh output.
